WN.lb(10,{"mt_9m2jSzGnvC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Slope −1/3 is perpendicular to slope…","choices":["3","−3","1/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip and change sign.","explain":"−1/3 × 3 = −1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Line through (0, {c}) parallel to y = {m}x + 1. What is its y-intercept?","answer":"c","hint":"y = mx + c.","explain":"c = {c}.","params":{"m":[2,6],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"y = 2x + 5 and y = −0.5x are…","choices":["perpendicular","parallel","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"2 × (−0.5).","explain":"2 × −0.5 = −1: perpendicular."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A line with slope {m} passes through (1, {b}). A parallel line passes through (0, 0). What is its y-value at x = 2?","answer":"2*m","hint":"y = mx.","explain":"y = {m} × 2 = {=2*m}.","params":{"m":[2,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"y = 4x and y = 4x are…","choices":["the same line","parallel and different","perpendicular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same slope and intercept.","explain":"Identical lines."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"y = x + 1 and y = −x + 1 are…","choices":["perpendicular","parallel","neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"1 × (−1).","explain":"1 × −1 = −1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"y = 2x and y = 3x are…","choices":["neither","parallel","perpendicular"],"answer":0,"hint":"Slopes differ, product 6.","explain":"Different slopes, product not −1."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A line has slope {m}. What slope does a parallel line have?","answer":"m","hint":"Same slope.","explain":"Parallel: {m}.","params":{"m":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A line has slope 2. Which slope is perpendicular?","choices":["−1/2","1/2","−2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip and change sign.","explain":"−1/2, because 2 × (−1/2) = −1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Line A has slope {m}. Line B is perpendicular. What is slope A × slope B?","answer":-1,"hint":"Always the same.","explain":"Perpendicular slopes multiply to −1.","params":{"m":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"y = 3x + 1 and y = 3x − 4 are…","choices":["parallel","perpendicular","the same line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare slopes.","explain":"Both slopes are 3, intercepts differ: parallel."}],"explain":{"kid":["Two lines that never meet are parallel. They have the same steepness, the same slope.","Lines that meet at a right angle are perpendicular. If one has slope 2, the other has slope −1/2."],"adult":["If y = m₁x + c₁ and y = m₂x + c₂, the lines are parallel when m₁ = m₂, and perpendicular when m₁ × m₂ = −1, so m₂ = −1/m₁: flip the fraction and change the sign.","To write a line through (a, b) parallel to y = 3x + 1, keep m = 3: y − b = 3(x − a). For a perpendicular line, use m = −1/3 instead. Vertical and horizontal lines are the special case with no defined product."]},"familyTip":"Draw y = 2x on squared paper, then a line through (0, 3) that crosses it at a right angle. Check its slope is −1/2."},"mt_hVyRYu6omo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Work out x² − 4x when x = −1.","answer":5,"hint":"Substitute in brackets: (−1)² − 4 × (−1). Then power, multiply, subtract.","explain":"(−1)² − 4 × (−1) = 1 − (−4) = 1 + 4 = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Work out −x² when x = {a}.","answer":"-(a*a)","hint":"Square the value first, then put the minus sign in front of the answer.","explain":"({a})² = {=a*a}, then the minus in front gives {=-(a*a)}.","params":{"a":[-8,-2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Work out {b}x + 7 when x = {a}.","answer":"b*a+7","hint":"{b}x means {b} × x. Multiply by the value in brackets, then add 7.","explain":"{b} × ({a}) + 7 = {=b*a} + 7 = {=b*a+7}.","params":{"a":[-5,-1],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A calculator shows −9 when you type − 3 x². Why?","choices":["It squares 3 first, then applies the minus: −3² = −(3²)","The calculator is wrong, because −3 squared is 9","Squaring a negative always gives a negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about which comes first: the power or the minus sign in front.","explain":"The power comes before a minus in front, so −3² = −(3 × 3) = −9. To square negative three, type the brackets: (−3)² = 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which keys make a calculator square negative three and show 9?","choices":["( − 3 ) x²","− 3 x²","3 x² −"],"answer":0,"hint":"The minus sign has to be inside the part that gets squared.","explain":"Brackets keep the minus with the 3, so (−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9. Without brackets the calculator works out −(3²) = −9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Work out 3 − x² when x = −5.","answer":-22,"hint":"Square −5 first. The square is positive, and then it is taken away from 3.","explain":"3 − (−5)² = 3 − 25 = −22. The square is 25, and taking 25 from 3 gives −22."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Work out 4x² − 1 when x = −1/2.","answer":0,"hint":"Square −1/2 first: multiply the tops and the bottoms. Then multiply by 4 and take away 1.","explain":"(−1/2)² = 1/4, so 4 × 1/4 − 1 = 1 − 1 = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam works out 2x² for x = −3 as (2 × −3)² = 36. What is right?","choices":["2x² = 2 × (−3)² = 18, because the power is only on x","36 is right","2x² = −18, because x is negative"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what the little 2 is attached to. Is it x, or 2x?","explain":"The power touches x only: (−3)² = 9, then 2 × 9 = 18. Sam squared 2x, which is a different expression, (2x)²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Work out (x + 1)² when x = {a}.","answer":"(a+1)*(a+1)","hint":"Brackets first: work out x + 1, then square that answer.","explain":"{a} + 1 = {=a+1}, and then ({=a+1})² = {=(a+1)*(a+1)}.","params":{"a":[-6,-2]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Work out x² when x = {a}.","answer":"a*a","hint":"Put the value in brackets and multiply it by itself. Count the minus signs.","explain":"({a})² = ({a}) × ({a}) = {=a*a}. A negative times a negative is positive.","params":{"a":[-9,-1]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is −x² when x = −4?","choices":["−16","16","8"],"answer":0,"hint":"The power comes before the minus in front. Find x² first, then make it negative.","explain":"x² = (−4)² = 16, and the minus in front makes it −16. Only (−x)² would give 16."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Work out 5 − 2x when x = {a}.","answer":"5-2*a","hint":"Write 5 − 2 × ({a}). Multiply first, then take the result away from 5.","explain":"5 − 2 × ({a}) = 5 − ({=2*a}) = {=5-2*a}. Taking away a negative adds.","params":{"a":[-6,-1]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Work out 2x² − 5x when x = −2.","answer":18,"hint":"The power is only on x. Work out (−2)² first, then each multiplication, then subtract.","explain":"2 × (−2)² − 5 × (−2) = 2 × 4 − (−10) = 8 + 10 = 18."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Work out x³ when x = {a}.","answer":"a*a*a","hint":"A cube multiplies three copies of the number. Count how many minus signs there are.","explain":"({a})³ = ({a}) × ({a}) × ({a}) = {=a*a*a}. Three negatives multiplied give a negative.","params":{"a":[-4,-1]}}],"explain":{"kid":["To evaluate an expression, swap each letter for its number. If the number is negative, write it in brackets. So 5 − 2x with x = −3 becomes 5 − 2 × (−3).","Then work it out in order. Do powers first. Next, multiply. Last, add and subtract, from left to right. Taking away a negative is like adding: 5 − (−6) = 5 + 6 = 11.","A negative times a negative is positive. So (−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9. A square can never be negative.","Watch a minus sign in front. −x² means: find x², then make it negative. With x = −3, that gives −9, not 9."],"adult":["Evaluating an expression means substituting a value for each variable and then simplifying with the usual order of operations: brackets, powers, multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction from left to right. Writing a substituted value in brackets, as in 2 × (−3), keeps its sign attached and shows each operation clearly.","A power binds tighter than a minus sign in front. So −x² means −(x²): with x = −3, x² = 9 and −x² = −9, while (−x)² = 9. Written numbers follow the same rule, so −3² = −9 but (−3)² = 9.","Even powers of a negative number are positive and odd powers are negative, because each pair of negative factors makes a positive: (−2)³ = −8 and (−2)⁴ = 16. Subtracting a negative adds its size, so 1 − (−4) = 5. Fractions work the same way: for x = −1/2, 4x² − 1 = 4 × 1/4 − 1 = 0.","Common mistakes: squaring a negative and keeping the minus sign, reading −x² as (−x)², losing the sign while substituting, turning a − (−b) into a − b, and treating 2x² as (2x)². On a calculator, type the brackets: without them, the calculator squares 3 first and shows −9 for −3²."]},"familyTip":"Pick a value together, such as x = −2, and each write down x², −x² and x³ before comparing answers. Then check on a calculator, once with brackets around −2 and once without, and talk about why the results differ."},"mt_d3V050rm1F":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Two numbers multiply to {=a*b} and add to {=a+b}. The smaller one is {a}. What is the larger one?","answer":"b","hint":"Find the number that goes with {a} to make {=a*b}. Then check the sum is {=a+b}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b} and {a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the larger number is {b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[5,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Expand (x − {a})(x − {b}). What is the constant term?","answer":"a*b","hint":"The constant term is the last number times the last number. Two negatives multiply to a positive.","explain":"(−{a}) × (−{b}) = {=a*b}, so the constant term is +{=a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,6],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Factorise x² − 9.","choices":["(x + 3)(x − 3)","(x − 3)(x − 3)","It cannot be factorised"],"answer":0,"hint":"Here b = 0. You need two numbers that multiply to −9 and add to 0.","explain":"3 × (−3) = −9 and 3 + (−3) = 0, so x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3). The x terms cancel."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ali says x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 5)(x + 6). What went wrong?","choices":["He copied b and c into the brackets","Nothing, it is right","He should have used (x − 5)(x − 6)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand (x + 5)(x + 6) and compare it with x² + 5x + 6.","explain":"(x + 5)(x + 6) = x² + 11x + 30. The numbers must multiply to 6 and add to 5, so the answer is (x + 2)(x + 3)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Factorise x² + 2x − 8.","choices":["(x + 4)(x − 2)","(x − 4)(x + 2)","(x + 8)(x − 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a pair that multiplies to −8 and adds to +2. The larger number takes the sign of b.","explain":"4 × (−2) = −8 and 4 + (−2) = 2. The pair −4 and 2 makes −8 too, but it adds to −2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which pair should you look for to factorise x² − x − 12?","choices":["Multiply to −12, add to −1","Multiply to −1, add to −12","Multiply to 12, add to 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"In x² + bx + c the pair multiplies to c and adds to b. Read b and c with their signs.","explain":"Here b = −1 and c = −12. The pair is −4 and 3, so x² − x − 12 = (x − 4)(x + 3)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"x² + 8x + 15 = (x + 3)(x + k). What is k?","answer":5,"hint":"3 times k must make 15, and 3 plus k must make 8.","explain":"3 × 5 = 15 and 3 + 5 = 8, so k = 5. Expanding (x + 3)(x + 5) gives x² + 8x + 15."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam factorises x² − x − 6 as (x + 3)(x − 2). How can Sam check it?","choices":["Expand it: x² + x − 6, so it is wrong","Put x = 0 in both: they agree, so it is right","It looks right, so no check is needed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the brackets out and compare every term, not just one value.","explain":"(x + 3)(x − 2) = x² + x − 6, so the middle sign is wrong. The answer is (x − 3)(x + 2). x = 0 only checks the constant."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x² + {=a+b}x + {=a*b} = (x + {a})(x + ?). What is the missing number?","answer":"b","hint":"The two numbers must multiply to {=a*b} and add to {=a+b}. Divide {=a*b} by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b} and {a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the missing number is {b}.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,7]},"where":"a<b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","choices":["(x + 3)(x + 4)","(x + 2)(x + 6)","(x + 7)(x + 12)"],"answer":0,"hint":"List the pairs that multiply to 12, then find the one that adds to 7.","explain":"3 × 4 = 12 and 3 + 4 = 7. The pair 2 and 6 also makes 12, but it adds to 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"x² + {=a-b}x − {=a*b} = (x + {a})(x − ?). What is the missing number?","answer":"b","hint":"The constant is negative, so the signs are different. You need {a} × (−?) = −{=a*b}.","explain":"{a} × (−{b}) = −{=a*b} and {a} + (−{b}) = {=a-b}, so the missing number is {b}.","params":{"a":[3,8],"b":[1,6]},"where":"a-b>1"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Factorise x² − 2x − 15.","choices":["(x − 5)(x + 3)","(x + 5)(x − 3)","(x − 5)(x − 3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The two numbers multiply to −15 and add to −2. Which one has to be negative?","explain":"−5 × 3 = −15 and −5 + 3 = −2. The pair 5 and −3 has the right product but adds to +2."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Factorise x² − 7x + 10.","choices":["(x − 2)(x − 5)","(x + 2)(x + 5)","(x + 2)(x − 5)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The constant is positive but the middle is negative. What does that say about both signs?","explain":"Both numbers are negative: −2 × −5 = 10 and −2 + −5 = −7. (x + 2)(x + 5) gives +7x."}],"explain":{"kid":["Factorising puts brackets back. It is expanding run in reverse. (x + 3)(x + 4) expands to x² + 7x + 12, so x² + 7x + 12 factorises to (x + 3)(x + 4).","Look at the last number first. The two numbers must multiply to it. For 12, the pairs are 1 and 12, 2 and 6, and 3 and 4.","Now look at the middle number. Pick the pair that adds to it. Only 3 and 4 add to 7, so the answer is (x + 3)(x + 4).","Signs matter. If the last number is negative, one number is negative and one is positive. If it is positive but the middle is negative, both are negative. So x² − 2x − 15 = (x − 5)(x + 3)."],"adult":["Expanding (x + p)(x + q) gives x² + (p + q)x + pq. So factorising x² + bx + c means finding integers p and q with p + q = b and pq = c. The order of the brackets does not matter: (x + 3)(x + 4) and (x + 4)(x + 3) are the same.","A reliable method is to list the factor pairs of c, including negative pairs, and test each sum. The signs narrow the search. If c is positive, p and q share the sign of b. If c is negative, they have opposite signs, and the larger size takes the sign of b.","Algebra tiles show why the method works. The x² tile and the c unit tiles fit into one rectangle only when the x strips split into p along one side and q along the other. Negative strips cancel positive ones in zero pairs, which is how x² + x − 6 can hold 3 positive and 2 negative strips.","Common mistakes: writing (x + b)(x + c), as in (x + 5)(x + 6) for x² + 5x + 6; choosing a pair with the right product but the wrong sum; flipping both signs; and thinking x² − 9 cannot be factorised because b = 0. It is (x + 3)(x − 3). Some expressions, such as x² + x + 1, do not factorise over the integers at all. Always expand your answer to check it."]},"familyTip":"Take turns choosing two whole numbers in secret, say 4 and −3, and tell the other person only their product and their sum (−12 and 1). They write the quadratic x² + x − 12 and factorise it. Then swap, and check by expanding."},"mt_ddCyW84sRo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"x − 4 = 6. What is x?","answer":10,"hint":"Add 4 to both sides.","explain":"x = 6 + 4 = 10."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"3x + 2 = 14. What is x?","answer":4,"hint":"Subtract 2 from both sides, then divide by 3.","explain":"3x = 12, so x = 4. Check: 3 × 4 + 2 = 14."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which step keeps 5 + x = 9 balanced?","choices":["Take 5 from both sides","Take 5 from the left side only","Add 5 to the left and take 5 from the right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both sides must change in the same way.","explain":"Taking 5 from both sides gives x = 4, and the equation stays balanced."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"x + {a} = {=a+b}. What is x?","answer":"b","hint":"Take {a} from both sides.","explain":"x = {=a+b} − {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{a}x = {=a*b}. What is x?","answer":"b","hint":"Divide both sides by {a}.","explain":"x = {=a*b} ÷ {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{a}x + {b} = {=a*c+b}. What is x?","answer":"c","hint":"Take {b} from both sides first.","explain":"Subtract {b}: {a}x = {=a*c}. Divide by {a}: x = {c}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"x − {a} = {b}. What is x?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add {a} to both sides.","explain":"x = {b} + {a} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x + {a} = {=a+b}. Find x.","answer":"b","hint":"Take {a} from both sides.","explain":"x = {=a+b} − {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is a good first step to solve x + 5 = 12?","choices":["Subtract 5 from both sides","Add 5 to both sides","Subtract 5 from the left side only"],"answer":0,"hint":"You want x on its own, and you must keep the balance.","explain":"Subtracting 5 from both sides gives x = 7 and keeps the equation balanced."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"2x = 10. What is x?","answer":5,"hint":"Divide both sides by 2.","explain":"x = 10 ÷ 2 = 5."},{"type":"order","id":"p4","prompt":"Put the steps in order to solve 2x + 1 = 9.","items":["2x + 1 = 9","2x = 8","x = 4","Check: 2 × 4 + 1 = 9"],"hint":"First undo the + 1, then undo the × 2.","explain":"Subtract 1 from both sides, divide both sides by 2, then check."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"{a}x = {=a*b}. Find x.","answer":"b","hint":"Divide both sides by {a}.","explain":"x = {=a*b} ÷ {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An equation is like balanced scales: both sides weigh the same. If x + 3 = 7, take 3 off both sides to keep it balanced, and you get x = 4.","The golden rule: whatever you do to one side, do to the other."],"adult":["An equation says two expressions are equal. Doing the same operation to both sides (adding, subtracting, multiplying, or dividing by a non-zero number) keeps them equal, so each step gives an equivalent equation.","Example: 2x + 1 = 9. Subtract 1 from both sides: 2x = 8. Divide both sides by 2: x = 4. Check by substituting: 2 × 4 + 1 = 9."]},"familyTip":"Play 'mystery bag': a bag with some counters plus 3 loose counters balances 7 counters. How many are in the bag?"},"mt_hTbTvblMUQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"If x = 4, what is 3x − 2?","answer":10,"hint":"3x means 3 × 4.","explain":"3 × 4 − 2 = 10."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Can x stand for different numbers in different problems?","choices":["Yes, a letter can stand for any number","No, x is always 10","No, x always means multiply"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a letter in algebra represents.","explain":"x can be any number. Within a single problem, the same letter keeps the same value."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is equal to a + a + a + a?","choices":["4a","a + 4","a4 (a number with digits a and 4)"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many a's are added?","explain":"Four lots of a is 4 × a, written 4a."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is {k}n?","answer":"k*n","hint":"{k}n means {k} × n.","explain":"{k} × {n} = {=k*n}.","params":{"n":[2,9],"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"If a = {a}, what is {b}a + {c}?","answer":"b*a+c","hint":"Work out {b}a first.","explain":"{b} × {a} + {c} = {=b*a+c}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[2,5],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"If x = {x}, what is x + x + x?","answer":"3*x","hint":"Replace each x with {x}.","explain":"{x} + {x} + {x} = {=3*x} (that's 3x).","params":{"x":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does ab mean?","choices":["a × b","a + b","A number with the digits a and b"],"answer":0,"hint":"Letters written next to each other are multiplied.","explain":"ab means a × b."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is 2n?","answer":"2*n","hint":"2n means 2 × n.","explain":"2 × {n} = {=2*n}.","params":{"n":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What does 4b mean?","choices":["4 × b","4 + b","A number in the forties"],"answer":0,"hint":"A number written next to a letter means multiply.","explain":"4b means 4 × b."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"If a = 3, what is a + a + a?","answer":9,"hint":"Replace each a with 3.","explain":"3 + 3 + 3 = 9 (and that's 3a)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"If a = 1, what is 3a + 2?","answer":5,"hint":"Work out 3a first.","explain":"3 × 1 + 2 = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"If a = {a}, what is a + a + a?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Replace each a with {a}.","explain":"{a} + {a} + {a} = {=3*a} (and that's 3a).","params":{"a":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In algebra a letter stands for a number. In 3a, a is a number and 3a means 3 × a. If a = 2, then 3a = 6.","The letter a doesn't mean 'apples'. It's a number we might not know yet, and in some problems it can change."],"adult":["A letter represents a number: sometimes one particular unknown, sometimes any value. The conventions are: 3a means 3 × a, ab means a × b, a/2 means a ÷ 2, and the same letter means the same number throughout one expression or equation.","A common misconception is treating the letter as an object ('3a is three apples'). That breaks down quickly: what would a × a mean?"]},"familyTip":"Make up 'think of a number' puzzles: 'I think of n, double it and add 1, and get 11.' What is n?"},"mt_i9rJbuFO3p":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"'A number plus {a} is {b}.' Which equation matches?","choices":["n + {a} = {b}","{a}n = {b}","n = {a} + {b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Plus' means +.","explain":"n + {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} boxes with the same number of pens hold {=a*k} pens in total. Let p be the pens in one box. Which equation matches?","choices":["{a}p = {=a*k}","p + {a} = {=a*k}","p = {a} × {=a*k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"{a} boxes of p pens is {a} × p.","explain":"{a}p = {=a*k}, so p = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"'I think of a number and subtract {a}. The answer is {b}.' Which equation matches?","choices":["n − {a} = {b}","{a} − n = {b}","n + {b} = {a}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with the number, then subtract {a}.","explain":"n − {a} = {b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"I think of a number, subtract {a} and get {b}. What was the number?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Undo the subtraction.","explain":"n − {a} = {b}, so n = {b} + {a} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"'Twice a number is 18.' Which equation matches?","choices":["2n = 18","n + 2 = 18","n² = 18"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Twice' means 2 ×.","explain":"2n = 18, so n = 9."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Before you write an equation for a word problem, what should you decide first?","choices":["What the letter stands for","What the answer is","Which operation is hardest"],"answer":0,"hint":"The letter needs a meaning.","explain":"Say what your letter stands for (for example, m = marbles in one bag) before writing the equation."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"'A number times 4 is 28.' What is the number?","answer":7,"hint":"4n = 28.","explain":"4n = 28, so n = 28 ÷ 4 = 7."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"'A number plus 7 is 12.' Which equation matches?","choices":["n + 7 = 12","7n = 12","n = 7 + 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Plus' means +, and 'is' means =.","explain":"n + 7 = 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"3 bags with the same number of marbles hold 24 marbles in total. Let m be the number in one bag. Which equation matches?","choices":["3m = 24","m + 3 = 24","m = 3 × 24"],"answer":0,"hint":"3 bags of m marbles is 3 × m.","explain":"3m = 24 (so m = 8)."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You had some money, spent £5, and have £8 left. Let x be what you started with. Which equation matches?","choices":["x − 5 = 8","5 − x = 8","x + 8 = 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with x, then take away what you spent.","explain":"x − 5 = 8 (so you started with £13)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In n + {a} = {b}, what is n?","answer":"b-a","hint":"What do you add to {a} to make {b}?","explain":"n = {b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[10,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"{a} bags with the same number of marbles hold {=a*k} marbles in total. Let m be the number in one bag. Which equation matches?","choices":["{a}m = {=a*k}","m + {a} = {=a*k}","m = {a} × {=a*k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"{a} bags of m marbles is {a} × m.","explain":"{a}m = {=a*k}, so m = {k}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"k":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A missing-number puzzle like □ + 7 = 12 can use a letter instead of a box: n + 7 = 12. The letter stands for the number you don't know yet.","To turn a word problem into an equation, pick a letter for the unknown, then write the facts with +, −, × or ÷ and an = sign."],"adult":["Writing an equation is translation: you name the unknown with a letter, then state the relationship the problem describes. 'I think of a number, add 7 and get 12' becomes n + 7 = 12.","Say what your letter means before you start (let m = marbles in one bag). Then check your equation by reading it back in words and seeing whether it matches the story."]},"familyTip":"Make up a 'think of a number' puzzle for someone else, write it as an equation, and see if they can solve it."},"mt_Jvg8X0N5u0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Perimeter = 2 × (length + width). Length {l} cm, width {w} cm. What is the perimeter in cm?","answer":"2*(l+w)","hint":"Brackets first.","explain":"2 × ({l} + {w}) = 2 × {=l+w} = {=2*(l+w)} cm.","params":{"l":[3,12],"w":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. Tickets cost £{p} each and you buy {q}. What is the total cost in £?","answer":"p*q","hint":"Multiply the price by the quantity.","explain":"£{p} × {q} = £{=p*q}.","params":{"p":[2,9],"q":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Distance = speed × time. A cyclist rides at {s} km per hour for {t} hours. How far do they go, in km?","answer":"s*t","hint":"Multiply speed by time.","explain":"{s} × {t} = {=s*t} km.","params":{"s":[8,20],"t":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Number of legs = 4 × number of dogs. How many legs do {n} dogs have?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Put {n} in place of 'number of dogs'.","explain":"4 × {n} = {=4*n} legs.","params":{"n":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Area = length × width. A rug is 3 m by 2 m. What is its area in m²?","answer":6,"hint":"Multiply the length by the width.","explain":"3 × 2 = 6 m²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. The price is 50p and the quantity is 4. What is the cost?","choices":["£2","£200","54p"],"answer":0,"hint":"50p × 4 = 200p. How many pounds is that?","explain":"50p × 4 = 200p = £2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Time = distance ÷ speed. You drive 90 miles at 30 miles per hour. How many hours does it take?","answer":3,"hint":"Divide the distance by the speed.","explain":"90 ÷ 30 = 3 hours."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Perimeter = 2 × (length + width). What is the perimeter of a {l} cm by {w} cm rectangle, in cm?","answer":"2*(l+w)","hint":"Add length and width first.","explain":"2 × ({l} + {w}) = 2 × {=l+w} = {=2*(l+w)} cm.","params":{"l":[3,10],"w":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. Pens cost £2 each and you buy 7. What is the cost in £?","answer":14,"hint":"Multiply the price by how many.","explain":"£2 × 7 = £14."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Time = distance ÷ speed. You travel 120 km at 60 km per hour. How many hours does it take?","answer":2,"hint":"Divide the distance by the speed.","explain":"120 ÷ 60 = 2 hours."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In perimeter = 2 × (length + width), what do you work out first?","choices":["length + width","2 × length","2 × width + length"],"answer":0,"hint":"Brackets first.","explain":"Work out length + width, then double it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Cost = price × quantity. Apples cost {p}p each and you buy {q}. What is the cost in pence?","answer":"p*q","hint":"Multiply the price by how many.","explain":"{p}p × {q} = {=p*q}p.","params":{"p":[10,40],"q":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A formula is a rule that tells you how to work something out. For a rectangle: perimeter = 2 × (length + width).","For a rectangle 5 cm long and 3 cm wide: 2 × (5 + 3) = 2 × 8 = 16 cm. Remember: brackets first!"],"adult":["Formulae package up a calculation you would otherwise redo every time: cost = price × quantity, perimeter = 2 × (length + width), time = distance ÷ speed. To use one, replace each word with its value, then calculate in the correct order.","Keep track of units. If the length is in cm, the perimeter is in cm. A cost in pence won't match a price in pounds unless you convert."]},"familyTip":"Measure a table or a book and use perimeter = 2 × (length + width) to find its perimeter. Then check with a tape measure."},"mt_KwdjWEmMNo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"If a = {a}, what is a²?","answer":"a*a","hint":"a² = a × a.","explain":"a² = {a} × {a} = {=a*a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"If a = {a} and b = {b}, what is ab?","answer":"a*b","hint":"ab = a × b.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the same as {k}y?","choices":["y added together {k} times","y to the power {k}","{k} + y"],"answer":0,"hint":"{k}y = {k} × y.","explain":"{k}y = {k} × y, which is y added together {k} times.","params":{"k":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"If x = {=2*h}, what is x/2?","answer":"h","hint":"x/2 means x ÷ 2.","explain":"{=2*h} ÷ 2 = {h}.","params":{"h":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is written the usual way in algebra?","choices":["3ab","ab3","a3b"],"answer":0,"hint":"Numbers go first.","explain":"By convention the number goes first: 3ab."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What does 2(x + 1) mean?","choices":["2 × (x + 1)","2x + 1","2 + x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"A number in front of a bracket multiplies everything in it.","explain":"2(x + 1) = 2 × (x + 1) = 2x + 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Is a² the same as 2a?","choices":["No: a² is a × a, but 2a is 2 × a","Yes, always","Only when a is even"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try a = 5.","explain":"With a = 5: a² = 25 but 2a = 10. They're different (they only match when a is 0 or 2)."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does 5y mean?","choices":["5 × y","5 + y","y to the power 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"A number next to a letter means multiply.","explain":"5y = 5 × y."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is the same as m × m?","choices":["m²","2m","m + m"],"answer":0,"hint":"A letter multiplied by itself is 'squared'.","explain":"m × m = m²."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you write 'p divided by 4'?","choices":["p/4","4p","4/p"],"answer":0,"hint":"Division is written as a fraction, with p on top.","explain":"p ÷ 4 = p/4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Work out a² when a = {a}.","answer":"a*a","hint":"a² = a × a.","explain":"{a} × {a} = {=a*a}.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"If p = {=4*h}, what is p/4?","answer":"h","hint":"p/4 means p ÷ 4.","explain":"{=4*h} ÷ 4 = {h}.","params":{"h":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["In algebra we write things the short way. 3y means 3 × y. ab means a × b. a/2 means a ÷ 2.","a² (say 'a squared') means a × a, not 2 × a. So if a = 5, then a² = 25 but 2a = 10."],"adult":["Algebraic notation is shorthand you need to read fluently: 3y = 3 × y = y + y + y; ab = a × b; a² = a × a; a/b = a ÷ b; x/2 is half of x; and brackets group, so 3(a + b) means 3 × (a + b).","Numbers go in front of letters (3y, not y3), and 1y is just written y. Watch for the classic trap: a² is a × a, not 2a."]},"familyTip":"Write your age as an expression in a sentence, such as 'In y years I'll be 13 + y', and get someone to read it back to you."},"mt_vHzVa3SURC":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"v = u + at. If u = {u}, a = {a} and t = {t}, what is v?","answer":"u+a*t","hint":"Multiply a × t first.","explain":"{a} × {t} = {=a*t}, then {u} + {=a*t} = {=u+a*t}.","params":{"u":[1,9],"a":[2,5],"t":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"P = 2l + 2w. If l = {l} and w = {w}, what is P?","answer":"2*l+2*w","hint":"2l means 2 × l.","explain":"2 × {l} + 2 × {w} = {=2*l} + {=2*w} = {=2*l+2*w}.","params":{"l":[2,9],"w":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A = ½bh. If b = {b} and h = {h}, what is A?","answer":"b*h/2","hint":"Multiply b and h, then halve.","explain":"{b} × {h} = {=b*h}, and half of that is {=b*h/2}.","params":{"b":[2,10],"h":[2,10]},"where":"(b*h)%2==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"If x = {x}, what is {k}x²?","answer":"k*x*x","hint":"Square first, then multiply.","explain":"{x}² = {=x*x}, then {k} × {=x*x} = {=k*x*x}.","params":{"x":[2,6],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"F = 9C/5 + 32. If C = {=5*k}, what is F?","answer":"9*k+32","hint":"Work out 9C/5 first.","explain":"9 × {=5*k} ÷ 5 = {=9*k}, then {=9*k} + 32 = {=9*k+32}.","params":{"k":[0,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"s = d/t. If d = 100 and t = 4, what is s?","answer":25,"hint":"d/t means d ÷ t.","explain":"100 ÷ 4 = 25."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"If a = −3, what is a²?","choices":["9","−9","−6"],"answer":0,"hint":"(−3) × (−3): a negative times a negative is positive.","explain":"(−3)² = (−3) × (−3) = 9."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"v = u + at. Find v when u = {u}, a = {a} and t = {t}.","answer":"u+a*t","hint":"Multiply a × t first.","explain":"{a} × {t} = {=a*t}, then {u} + {=a*t} = {=u+a*t}.","params":{"u":[1,9],"a":[2,5],"t":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"P = 2l + 2w. If l = 5 and w = 3, what is P?","answer":16,"hint":"2l means 2 × l.","explain":"2 × 5 + 2 × 3 = 10 + 6 = 16."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A = ½bh. If b = 6 and h = 4, what is A?","answer":12,"hint":"Multiply b and h, then halve.","explain":"6 × 4 = 24, and half of 24 is 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"If x = 4, what is 3x²?","choices":["48","144","24"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square x first, then multiply by 3.","explain":"x² = 16, and 3 × 16 = 48. (144 would be (3x)².)"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"P = 2l + 2w. Find P when l = {l} and w = {w}.","answer":"2*l+2*w","hint":"2l means 2 × l.","explain":"2 × {l} + 2 × {w} = {=2*l} + {=2*w} = {=2*l+2*w}.","params":{"l":[2,9],"w":[2,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Substituting means swapping each letter for its number. If v = u + at, with u = 2, a = 3 and t = 4, then v = 2 + 3 × 4 = 14.","Put the numbers in, then use the order of operations: multiply before you add."],"adult":["Substitution is how you use formulae in science and maths: replace each letter with its value, then evaluate using the order of operations. For v = u + at with u = 2, a = 3 and t = 4: v = 2 + 3 × 4 = 14.","Writing out the substituted line in full, with brackets around negative values, prevents most errors. With a = −2, 3a² means 3 × (−2)² = 3 × 4 = 12."]},"familyTip":"Use F = 9C/5 + 32 to convert today's temperature from °C to °F, and compare it with a weather app."},"mt_FX4a2Q8XXN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Expand {a}(x + {b}).","choices":["{a}x + {=a*b}","{a}x + {b}","x + {=a*b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by {a}.","explain":"{a} × x + {a} × {b} = {a}x + {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a}(x + {b}) = {a}x + ?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply {b} by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Expand {a}({b}x − {c}).","choices":["{=a*b}x − {=a*c}","{=a*b}x − {c}","{=a+b}x − {=a*c}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b}x = {=a*b}x and {a} × {c} = {=a*c}, so you get {=a*b}x − {=a*c}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6],"c":[1,9]},"where":"a*b!=a+b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Check by substituting: if x = {x}, what is {a}(x + {b})?","answer":"a*(x+b)","hint":"Work out the bracket first.","explain":"{a} × ({x} + {b}) = {a} × {=x+b} = {=a*(x+b)}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,9],"x":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Expand 5(2a + 3b).","choices":["10a + 15b","10a + 3b","7a + 8b"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by 5.","explain":"5 × 2a = 10a and 5 × 3b = 15b."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which expansion is correct?","choices":["6(x − 1) = 6x − 6","6(x − 1) = 6x − 1","6(x − 1) = 6x + 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by 6.","explain":"6 × x − 6 × 1 = 6x − 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Expand −3(y + 2).","choices":["−3y − 6","−3y + 6","−3y + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"−3 × +2 is negative.","explain":"−3 × y = −3y and −3 × 2 = −6."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Expand 3(x + 4).","choices":["3x + 12","3x + 4","x + 12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both x and 4 by 3.","explain":"3 × x + 3 × 4 = 3x + 12."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Expand 2(5 − y).","choices":["10 − 2y","10 − y","7 − 2y"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both 5 and y by 2.","explain":"2 × 5 − 2 × y = 10 − 2y."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"{a}(x + {b}) = {a}x + □. What number goes in the box?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply {b} by {a}.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}, so {a}(x + {b}) = {a}x + {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Expand −2(x − 3).","choices":["−2x + 6","−2x − 6","−2x − 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"−2 × −3 is positive.","explain":"−2 × x = −2x and −2 × −3 = +6, so you get −2x + 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Expand {a}(x − {b}).","choices":["{a}x − {=a*b}","{a}x − {b}","{a}x + {=a*b}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply both terms by {a}, and keep the minus sign.","explain":"{a} × x − {a} × {b} = {a}x − {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["To expand a bracket, multiply each thing inside by the number outside. 3(x + 4) = 3 × x + 3 × 4 = 3x + 12.","Think of 3 bags, each holding x sweets plus 4 more sweets: altogether you have 3x + 12 sweets."],"adult":["Expanding uses the distributive law: a(b + c) = ab + ac. So 3(2x + 5) = 6x + 15, and 4(x − 2) = 4x − 8.","The most common slip is multiplying only the first term: 3(x + 4) is not 3x + 4. With a negative outside, every sign inside flips: −2(x − 3) = −2x + 6."]},"familyTip":"Check an expansion by substituting a number: if x = 2, then 3(x + 4) = 18 and 3x + 12 = 18. If the two disagree, there's a mistake."},"mt_aZENxGikHn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which expression means '{k} less than n'?","choices":["n − {k}","{k} − n","{k}n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with n and take away {k}.","explain":"n − {k}.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which expression means '{c} more than {k} times a number n'?","choices":["{k}n + {c}","{k}(n + {c})","{c}n + {k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"Build it from the inside: {k} times n first.","explain":"{k}n + {c}.","params":{"k":[2,9],"c":[1,9]},"where":"k!=c"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is the value of '{c} more than {k} times n'?","answer":"k*n+c","hint":"Write it as {k}n + {c}, then substitute.","explain":"{k} × {n} + {c} = {=k*n+c}.","params":{"k":[2,9],"c":[1,9],"n":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which expression means 'n divided by {k}'?","choices":["n/{k}","{k}/n","n − {k}"],"answer":0,"hint":"n goes on top.","explain":"n ÷ {k} = n/{k}.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which expression means 'the product of a and b'?","choices":["ab","a + b","a/b"],"answer":0,"hint":"'Product' means multiply.","explain":"ab = a × b."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which expression means 'twice the sum of x and 3'?","choices":["2(x + 3)","2x + 3","x + 3 + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the sum first, then double it.","explain":"The sum is x + 3; twice that is 2(x + 3)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Sam is s years old. His sister is 4 years younger. Which expression gives her age?","choices":["s − 4","4 − s","s + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Younger means fewer years.","explain":"s − 4."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which expression means '{k} more than n'?","choices":["n + {k}","{k}n","{k} − n"],"answer":0,"hint":"'More than' means add.","explain":"n + {k}.","params":{"k":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which expression means '3 less than n'?","choices":["n − 3","3 − n","3n"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with n, then take 3 away.","explain":"n − 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which expression means '5 more than twice a number n'?","choices":["2n + 5","2(n + 5)","5n + 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twice n is 2n. Then add 5.","explain":"2n + 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which expression means 'a number n shared equally between 4 people'?","choices":["n/4","4/n","n − 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sharing means dividing n.","explain":"n ÷ 4 = n/4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"If n = {n}, what is the value of 'twice n, minus {c}'?","answer":"2*n-c","hint":"Write it as 2n − {c}, then substitute.","explain":"2 × {n} − {c} = {=2*n-c}.","params":{"n":[3,9],"c":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["You can turn words into algebra. If n is a number, '5 more than n' is n + 5, 'twice n' is 2n, and '3 less than n' is n − 3.","Careful with 'less than': '3 less than n' means start with n and take 3 away. That's n − 3, not 3 − n."],"adult":["Turning words into expressions is mostly vocabulary: 'more than' or 'increased by' means +; 'less than' or 'decreased by' means −; 'twice' or 'the product of' means ×; 'shared between' or 'the quotient of' means ÷.","Order matters for − and ÷: '7 less than n' is n − 7, and 'n divided by 3' is n/3. For combined phrases, build from the inside out: '5 more than twice a number' is 2n + 5."]},"familyTip":"Describe a phone plan or bus fare in words (such as '£10 a month plus £2 per extra GB'), then write it as an expression."},"mt_QhFEDyIwSO":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Solve {a}x + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Subtract {b}, then divide by {a}.","explain":"{a}x = {=a*x}, so x = {x}. Check: {a} × {x} + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,15],"x":[1,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Solve {a}x − {b} = {=a*x-b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Add {b} to both sides first.","explain":"{a}x = {=a*x}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,15],"x":[2,10]},"where":"a*x>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Solve {a}(x + {b}) = {=a*(x+b)}.","answer":"x","hint":"Divide both sides by {a} first.","explain":"x + {b} = {=x+b}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,6],"x":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Solve {a}x + {b} = {c}x + {=(a-c)*x+b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Subtract {c}x from both sides first.","explain":"{=a-c}x + {b} = {=(a-c)*x+b}, so {=a-c}x = {=(a-c)*x} and x = {x}.","params":{"a":[5,9],"c":[2,4],"b":[1,9],"x":[1,9]},"where":"a-c>=2"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Solve x/{d} + {b} = {=k+b}.","answer":"d*k","hint":"Subtract {b}, then multiply by {d}.","explain":"x/{d} = {k}, so x = {k} × {d} = {=d*k}.","params":{"d":[2,5],"k":[1,6],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which step is correct for 4x − 5 = 23?","choices":["Add 5 to both sides to get 4x = 28","Subtract 5 from both sides to get 4x = 18","Divide only 4x by 4 to get x − 5 = 23"],"answer":0,"hint":"Undo the − 5, and do it to both sides.","explain":"Adding 5 to both sides gives 4x = 28, so x = 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Solve 7 − x = 2.","answer":5,"hint":"What do you take from 7 to leave 2?","explain":"7 − 5 = 2, so x = 5. (Or add x to both sides: 7 = 2 + x.)"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Find x: {a}x + {b} = {=a*x+b}.","answer":"x","hint":"Subtract {b} from both sides, then divide by {a}.","explain":"{a}x = {=a*x}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,9],"x":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Solve 5x − 7 = 18.","answer":5,"hint":"Add 7 to both sides first.","explain":"5x = 25, so x = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Solve 3(x + 1) = 18.","answer":5,"hint":"Expand, or divide both sides by 3 first.","explain":"x + 1 = 6, so x = 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Solve 5x + 2 = 2x + 14.","answer":4,"hint":"Subtract 2x from both sides first.","explain":"3x + 2 = 14, then 3x = 12, so x = 4."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Find x: {a}(x − {b}) = {=a*(x-b)}.","answer":"x","hint":"Divide both sides by {a} first.","explain":"x − {b} = {=x-b}, so x = {x}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4],"x":[2,9]},"where":"x>b"}],"explain":{"kid":["To solve 2x + 3 = 11, undo the + 3 first (take 3 from both sides: 2x = 8), then undo the × 2 (divide both sides by 2: x = 4).","If there's a bracket, you can expand it first: 3(x + 1) = 18 becomes 3x + 3 = 18."],"adult":["Solving a linear equation means isolating the unknown using operations that keep both sides equal. Undo things in reverse order: for 5x − 7 = 18, add 7 (5x = 25), then divide by 5 (x = 5).","When x appears on both sides, gather the x terms on one side first: 5x + 2 = 2x + 14 → 3x + 2 = 14 → 3x = 12 → x = 4. Always check by substituting back into the original equation."]},"familyTip":"After solving, substitute your answer back into the original equation. If both sides match, you know you're right without looking at the answers."},"mt_mmUVPwnv3o":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A taxi charges £{f} plus £{r} per mile. The fare was £{=f+r*m}. How many miles was the trip?","answer":"m","hint":"Write {f} + {r}m = {=f+r*m}.","explain":"{r}m = {=r*m}, so m = {m}. Check: {f} + {r} × {m} = {=f+r*m} ✓.","params":{"f":[2,5],"r":[2,4],"m":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Mia has some sweets. She gets {a} more and now has {=s+a}. How many did she have at first?","answer":"s","hint":"s + {a} = {=s+a}.","explain":"s = {=s+a} − {a} = {s}.","params":{"a":[3,9],"s":[5,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} friends share a bill equally and each pays £{k}. What was the bill in £?","answer":"n*k","hint":"b ÷ {n} = {k}.","explain":"b = {k} × {n} = {=n*k}.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[5,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A rectangle's length is {d} cm more than its width. Its perimeter is {=4*w+2*d} cm. What is the width in cm?","answer":"w","hint":"Perimeter = 2(w + w + {d}) = 4w + {=2*d}.","explain":"4w + {=2*d} = {=4*w+2*d}, so 4w = {=4*w} and w = {w}.","params":{"w":[2,10],"d":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Two consecutive whole numbers add up to {=2*x+1}. What is the smaller one?","answer":"x","hint":"Call them n and n + 1.","explain":"n + n + 1 = {=2*x+1}, so 2n = {=2*x} and n = {x}.","params":{"x":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You're solving a problem about the number of people on a bus and get x = −4. What should you do?","choices":["Recheck the equation: a negative number of people doesn't make sense","Write −4 as the answer","Round it to 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the answer fit the story?","explain":"Answers must make sense in context. A negative count means the equation or the working has a mistake."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Ali is 3 times as old as his sister. Together they are 32. How old is his sister?","answer":8,"hint":"Let her age be s. Then Ali is 3s, and s + 3s = 32.","explain":"4s = 32, so s = 8 (and Ali is 24). Check: 8 + 24 = 32 ✓."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Tom has some stickers. He gets {a} more and now has {=s+a}. How many did he have at the start?","answer":"s","hint":"s + {a} = {=s+a}.","explain":"s = {=s+a} − {a} = {s}. Check: {s} + {a} = {=s+a} ✓.","params":{"a":[3,9],"s":[5,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A taxi charges £3 plus £2 per mile. The fare was £17. How many miles was the trip?","answer":7,"hint":"3 + 2m = 17.","explain":"2m = 14, so m = 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Three friends share a bill equally and each pays £12. What was the total bill in £?","answer":36,"hint":"b ÷ 3 = 12.","explain":"b = 12 × 3 = 36."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A rectangle's length is 3 cm more than its width. Its perimeter is 26 cm. What is the width in cm?","answer":5,"hint":"Let the width be w. Then the length is w + 3, and the perimeter is 2(w + w + 3).","explain":"2(2w + 3) = 26, so 4w + 6 = 26, 4w = 20 and w = 5. Check: 2 × (5 + 8) = 26 ✓."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A gym charges a £{f} joining fee plus £{r} a week. Sam paid £{=f+r*w} in total. How many weeks was that?","answer":"w","hint":"Write {f} + {r}w = {=f+r*w}.","explain":"{r}w = {=r*w}, so w = {w}. Check: {f} + {r} × {w} = {=f+r*w} ✓.","params":{"f":[10,30],"r":[5,9],"w":[2,10]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Word problems get easier once you write an equation. 'Tom has some stickers. He gets 6 more and now has 20.' Let s be Tom's stickers at the start: s + 6 = 20, so s = 14.","Always check: 14 + 6 = 20 ✓. Also ask whether the answer makes sense: you can't have −3 stickers."],"adult":["A reliable routine: (1) say what the letter stands for, (2) write the equation from the story, (3) solve it, (4) check it against the original words, not just your equation.","Example: a taxi charges £3 plus £2 per mile and the fare was £17. Let m = miles: 3 + 2m = 17, so 2m = 14 and m = 7. Check: £3 + 7 × £2 = £17 ✓."]},"familyTip":"Find a real pricing rule (a taxi, a gym, a phone plan) and work out the unknown from a total, such as how many miles a £20 fare covers."},"mt_q0LJXMQe70":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line through ({p}, {q}) and ({=p+r}, {=q+m*r}).","answer":"m","hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise {=m*r} ÷ run {r} = {m}.","params":{"p":[0,5],"q":[0,5],"r":[1,4],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line through (0, {=q+m*r}) and ({r}, {q}).","answer":"0-m","hint":"y goes down, so the gradient is negative.","explain":"Rise {q} − {=q+m*r} = −{=m*r}, run {r}, so the gradient is −{m}.","params":{"q":[0,5],"r":[1,4],"m":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the gradient of a horizontal line?","choices":["0","1","It has no gradient"],"answer":0,"hint":"How much does y change?","explain":"y doesn't change, so rise = 0 and the gradient is 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A line has gradient {m} and passes through ({x}, {=m*x+c}). Find c.","answer":"c","hint":"Substitute into y = {m}x + c.","explain":"{=m*x+c} = {m} × {x} + c = {=m*x} + c, so c = {c}.","params":{"m":[2,5],"x":[1,4],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which equation is the line with gradient 3 that crosses the y-axis at −4?","choices":["y = 3x − 4","y = −4x + 3","y = 3x + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = mx + c.","explain":"m = 3 and c = −4, so y = 3x − 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Ana says the gradient from (2, 1) to (6, 9) is 4 ÷ 8 = 0.5. What went wrong?","choices":["She did run ÷ rise; it should be 8 ÷ 4 = 2","She should add, not divide","Nothing: 0.5 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which goes on top?","explain":"Rise is 9 − 1 = 8 and run is 6 − 2 = 4. Gradient = rise ÷ run = 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A line goes down from left to right. Its gradient is…","choices":["negative","positive","zero"],"answer":0,"hint":"As x increases, y decreases.","explain":"y falls as x rises, so the gradient is negative."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line through (1, 3) and (3, 7).","answer":2,"hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise 7 − 3 = 4 and run 3 − 1 = 2, so the gradient is 4 ÷ 2 = 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the gradient of the line from (0, {b}) to ({r}, {=b+m*r}).","answer":"m","hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise {=m*r}, run {r}, so the gradient is {=m*r} ÷ {r} = {m}.","params":{"b":[0,9],"r":[1,5],"m":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"In y = 5x − 2, what is the gradient?","choices":["5","−2","5x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare with y = mx + c.","explain":"m is the number in front of x, so the gradient is 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A line has gradient {m} and passes through (1, {=m+c}). Find c in y = mx + c.","answer":"c","hint":"Put x = 1 and y = {=m+c} into y = {m}x + c.","explain":"{=m+c} = {m} × 1 + c, so c = {c}.","params":{"m":[2,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which line is steeper: y = {a}x + 1 or y = {b}x + 9?","choices":["y = {a}x + 1","y = {b}x + 9","They are equally steep"],"answer":0,"hint":"Steepness comes from m, not c.","explain":"The gradients are {a} and {b}. The +9 only moves the line up; it doesn't make it steeper.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[1,8]},"where":"a>b"}],"explain":{"kid":["Gradient tells you how steep a line is. Pick two points. Gradient = change in y ÷ change in x. From (1, 3) to (3, 7), y goes up 4 while x goes up 2. So the gradient is 4 ÷ 2 = 2.","Every straight line can be written as y = mx + c. Here m is the gradient. c is where the line crosses the y-axis. This line is y = 2x + 1."],"adult":["Gradient = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁), or rise over run. A line going down from left to right has a negative gradient. A flat line has gradient 0.","To find the equation, work out m. Then put one point into y = mx + c to find c. Through (1, 3) with m = 2: 3 = 2 + c, so c = 1 and y = 2x + 1. In the US this is often written y = mx + b."]},"familyTip":"Find a ramp or a step. Measure how far it rises and how far it runs, then work out its gradient."},"mt_yYs2sYPwjl":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the leading coefficient of {c} + {b}x + {a}x²?","answer":"a","hint":"Write it in standard form first.","explain":"Standard form is {a}x² + {b}x + {c}, so the leading coefficient is {a}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9],"c":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is 5 − 3x² + 2x in standard form?","choices":["−3x² + 2x + 5","5 + 2x − 3x²","2x − 3x² + 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Highest power first.","explain":"x² first, then x, then the number: −3x² + 2x + 5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the degree of {a}x^{n} − x^{k}?","answer":"n","hint":"The biggest power wins.","explain":"The powers are {n} and {k}, so the degree is {n}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"n":[4,7],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is 7 called?","choices":["A monomial of degree 0","A binomial","Not a polynomial"],"answer":0,"hint":"One term, no x.","explain":"A single number is a monomial with degree 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is NOT a polynomial?","choices":["1/x + 2","x² − 1","5x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Powers must be whole and not negative.","explain":"1/x is x⁻¹, a negative power, so it is not a polynomial."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A degree-2 polynomial is called…","choices":["quadratic","linear","cubic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Degree 1 is linear, 3 is cubic.","explain":"Degree 2 is quadratic."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"How many terms are in x^{n} + x^{k} + x + {c}?","answer":"4","hint":"Count them.","explain":"There are 4 terms.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[2,4],"c":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many terms does 4x³ − x + 7 have?","choices":["3","2","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the parts joined by + or −.","explain":"4x³, −x and 7: three terms, so it is a trinomial."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is the degree of {a}x^{n} + {b}x + 1?","answer":"n","hint":"Highest power of x.","explain":"The highest power is {n}, so the degree is {n}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"n":[3,6],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"What is the constant term of {a}x² − 3x + {c}?","answer":"c","hint":"The plain number.","explain":"The constant term is {c}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"c":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is a binomial?","choices":["x² + 5","3x","x² + x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bi means two.","explain":"x² + 5 has two terms."}],"explain":{"kid":["A term is a number, a letter, or both multiplied, like 3x². Terms are joined by + or −.","One term is a monomial. Two terms make a binomial. Three make a trinomial. The degree is the biggest power of x."],"adult":["Standard form writes the terms from the highest power down: 5 − 3x² + 2x becomes −3x² + 2x + 5.","In standard form the first number is the leading coefficient (−3), and the plain number is the constant term (5). The degree is 2, so this is a quadratic trinomial."]},"familyTip":"Write a few expressions on cards. Take turns naming each one: how many terms, what degree?"},"mt_93GREOFJAe":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Simplify 2^{a} × 2^{b} as 2 to what power?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Same base, add.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Simplify y^{a} ÷ y^{b}. What is the power?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Same base, subtract.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[9,15],"b":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Simplify (a^{p})^{q}. What is the power?","answer":"p*q","hint":"Multiply.","explain":"{p} × {q} = {=p*q}.","params":{"p":[2,6],"q":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which can the laws NOT combine into one power?","choices":["2³ × 3²","2³ × 2²","5⁴ ÷ 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Bases must match.","explain":"2 and 3 are different bases."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{c}x^{a} × {d}x^{b} = kx^n. What is k?","answer":"c*d","hint":"Multiply the numbers, add the powers.","explain":"{c} × {d} = {=c*d}.","params":{"c":[2,6],"d":[2,6],"a":[2,5],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"x⁷ ÷ x⁷ = ?","choices":["1","0","x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Anything divided by itself.","explain":"Every factor cancels: the answer is 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"(x²)³ is…","choices":["x⁶","x⁵","x⁸"],"answer":0,"hint":"Three lots of x².","explain":"x² × x² × x² = x⁶."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x^{a} × x^{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the powers.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"x^{a} ÷ x^{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract the powers.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[8,12],"b":[2,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"(x^{a})^{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply the powers.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why is x³ × x⁵ not x¹⁵?","choices":["There are only 3 + 5 = 8 factors of x","Because 15 is too big","You should subtract"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the x's.","explain":"3 x's and 5 x's make 8 x's: x⁸."}],"explain":{"kid":["x³ means x × x × x. The small number counts how many x's are multiplied.","x³ × x⁵ puts 3 x's next to 5 more. That is 8 x's, so it is x⁸. You add the powers."],"adult":["Multiply: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ. Divide: aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, because n factors cancel. Power of a power: (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ.","The laws need the same base. 2³ × 3² cannot be combined this way. A common slip is x³ × x⁵ = x¹⁵. Counting the factors shows it is x⁸."]},"familyTip":"Write x³ × x⁵ the long way, with every x. Count them together."},"mt_mq4eGlJDk9":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{b}^-1 = 1/? (give the denominator)","answer":"b","hint":"Flip it.","explain":"{b}⁻¹ = 1/{b}.","params":{"b":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"4^-{n} = 1/? (give the denominator)","answer":"4**n","hint":"4ⁿ underneath.","explain":"4^{n} = {=4**n}.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"10⁻² = ?","choices":["0.01","−100","0.2"],"answer":0,"hint":"1/10².","explain":"1/100 = 0.01."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why is a⁰ = 1?","choices":["a³ ÷ a³ is a⁰ and also 1","Zero times anything is 0","It is just a rule with no reason"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the divide law.","explain":"a³ ÷ a³ = a³⁻³ = a⁰, and any number ÷ itself = 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"x^{a} × x^-{b} = x^? What is the power?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Add the powers.","explain":"{a} + (−{b}) = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"What is 2^-{n} × 2^{n}?","answer":"1","hint":"Powers add to 0.","explain":"−{n} + {n} = 0, and 2⁰ = 1.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is the largest?","choices":["2⁰","2⁻¹","2⁻³"],"answer":0,"hint":"1, 1/2, 1/8.","explain":"2⁰ = 1 is the largest."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"5⁰ = ?","choices":["1","0","5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Follow the pattern.","explain":"Any non-zero number to the power 0 is 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"2^-{n} = 1/? (give the denominator)","answer":"2**n","hint":"2⁻ⁿ = 1/2ⁿ.","explain":"2^{n} = {=2**n}, so the answer is 1/{=2**n}.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"10^-{n} as a decimal: how many places after the point is the 1?","answer":"n","hint":"10⁻¹ = 0.1.","explain":"10⁻{n} has the 1 in place {n}.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Is 3⁻² negative?","choices":["No, it is 1/9","Yes, it is −9","Yes, it is −6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Negative power, not negative number.","explain":"3⁻² = 1/9, a small positive number."}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at 2³ = 8, 2² = 4, 2¹ = 2. Each time the power goes down by 1, we halve.","Halve once more: 2⁰ = 1. Then 2⁻¹ = 1/2 and 2⁻² = 1/4. A minus power means a fraction."],"adult":["Going down the pattern divides by the base each time, so a⁰ = 1 for any a that is not 0. The laws agree: a³ ÷ a³ = a⁰, and it also equals 1.","Continue the pattern: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. So 10⁻² = 1/100 = 0.01 and 2⁻³ = 1/8. A negative power is small but still positive."]},"familyTip":"Write the powers of 10 down a page, from 1000 to 0.001. Label each with its power."},"mt_OcSj7J9q1t":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"Find the square root of {=r*r}, written as a power: {=r*r}^(1/2).","answer":"r","hint":"Square root.","explain":"√{=r*r} = {r}.","params":{"r":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"What is {=r*r*r}^(2/3)?","answer":"r*r","hint":"Cube root, then square.","explain":"∛{=r*r*r} = {r} and {r}² = {=r*r}.","params":{"r":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Write ∛x as a power.","choices":["x^(1/3)","x³","x/3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cube root is 1/3.","explain":"∛x = x^(1/3)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"16^(3/4) = ?","choices":["8","12","64"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fourth root first.","explain":"16^(1/4) = 2 and 2³ = 8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"x^(1/2) × x^(1/2) = x^? Give the power.","answer":1,"hint":"Add the powers.","explain":"1/2 + 1/2 = 1, so x¹ = x."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why is 25^(1/2) = 5?","choices":["(25^(1/2))² = 25¹ = 25, and 5² = 25","25 ÷ 2 is about 5","Half of 10 is 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Power of a power.","explain":"Squaring 25^(1/2) gives 25, so it must be 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Write x^(3/2) with a root.","choices":["(√x)³","∛(x²)","3√x/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Denominator is the root.","explain":"The 2 means a square root, the 3 means cubed: (√x)³."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"What is {=r*r}^(1/2)?","answer":"r","hint":"Which number squared gives it?","explain":"{r} × {r} = {=r*r}, so the answer is {r}.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"What is {=r*r*r}^(1/3)?","answer":"r","hint":"Which number cubed gives it?","explain":"{r}³ = {=r*r*r}, so the answer is {r}.","params":{"r":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"x^(1/2) is the same as…","choices":["√x","x/2","x²"],"answer":0,"hint":"A half power.","explain":"A power of 1/2 is a square root."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"8^(2/3) = ?","choices":["4","16","5.33"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cube root first, then square.","explain":"∛8 = 2 and 2² = 4."}],"explain":{"kid":["9 = 3 × 3. So 3 is the square root of 9. We can also write it as 9 to the power 1/2.","27 = 3 × 3 × 3. So the cube root of 27 is 3, and 27 to the power 1/3 is 3."],"adult":["Use the power-of-a-power law: (a^(1/n))ⁿ = a¹ = a. So a^(1/n) is the number that gives a when it is multiplied n times: the nth root.","For a^(m/n), take the root first, then the power: 8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 2² = 4. Taking the root first keeps the numbers small."]},"familyTip":"Find numbers that are perfect squares and cubes, like 64. Then try 64 to the powers 1/2, 1/3 and 2/3."},"mt_iHEB0g_nCc":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"x² = {=r*r}. What is the negative solution?","answer":"-r","hint":"Use −√p.","explain":"x = −√{=r*r} = −{r}.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"x³ = -{=r*r*r}. What is x?","answer":"-r","hint":"Cubes keep the sign.","explain":"∛(−{=r*r*r}) = −{r}.","params":{"r":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"√2 is…","choices":["irrational","exactly 1.4","a whole number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its decimal never stops.","explain":"√2 = 1.41421… never ends or repeats: irrational."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"x² = {=r*r}. What is the sum of the two solutions?","answer":"0","hint":"They are opposites.","explain":"{r} + (−{r}) = 0.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Solve x³ = 1000.","choices":["x = 10","x = ±10","x = 100"],"answer":0,"hint":"One cube root.","explain":"10³ = 1000, and only one real answer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"On a graph of y = x², the line y = 9 crosses at…","choices":["x = −3 and x = 3","x = 3 only","x = 81"],"answer":0,"hint":"The curve is symmetric.","explain":"Both ±3 give 9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A square has area {=r*r} cm². How long is its side, in cm?","answer":"r","hint":"Length can't be negative.","explain":"√{=r*r} = {r} cm (ignore −{r} for a length).","params":{"r":[3,15]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"x² = {=r*r}. What is the positive solution?","answer":"r","hint":"Square root.","explain":"√{=r*r} = {r}, so x = ±{r}; the positive one is {r}.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"x³ = {=r*r*r}. What is x?","answer":"r","hint":"Cube root.","explain":"∛{=r*r*r} = {r}.","params":{"r":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many real solutions does x² = 49 have?","choices":["2","1","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"7 and…?","explain":"7 and −7: two solutions."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"x² = −4 has…","choices":["no real solutions","x = −2","x = ±2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a square be negative?","explain":"A square is never negative, so there is no real solution."}],"explain":{"kid":["6 × 6 = 36. But −6 × −6 = 36 too! So x² = 36 has two answers: 6 and −6.","For cubes, 3 × 3 × 3 = 27, but −3 cubed is −27. So x³ = 27 has just one answer, 3."],"adult":["To solve x² = p, take the square root of both sides and keep both signs: x = ±√p. If p is negative there is no real answer, because a square is never negative.","To solve x³ = p, take the cube root: x = ∛p. Cubes keep the sign, so ∛(−8) = −2. Some roots, like √2, are irrational. Their decimals never end or repeat."]},"familyTip":"Draw y = x² and a flat line y = 9 on squared paper. Where do they meet?"},"mt_Vn2yQGKnvi":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"(x − {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is c?","answer":"-a*b","hint":"Watch the sign.","explain":"−{a} × {b} = {=-a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"(x − {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is k?","answer":"b-a","hint":"−{a}x + {b}x.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"({p}x + 1)(x + {q}) = ax² + bx + c. What is a?","answer":"p","hint":"x² terms only.","explain":"{p}x × x = {p}x².","params":{"p":[2,6],"q":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"(x + 1)³ = ?","choices":["x³ + 3x² + 3x + 1","x³ + 1","x³ + 3x + 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply two brackets first.","explain":"(x + 1)² = x² + 2x + 1; times (x + 1) gives x³ + 3x² + 3x + 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"What degree is (x² + 1)(x³ − 2)?","choices":["5","6","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Highest powers multiply.","explain":"x² × x³ = x⁵, degree 5."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"(x + 3)² = ?","choices":["x² + 6x + 9","x² + 9","x² + 3x + 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"(x + 3)(x + 3).","explain":"x² + 3x + 3x + 9 = x² + 6x + 9."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"(x + {a})(x − {a}) = x² + kx + c. What is k?","answer":"0","hint":"The middle terms cancel.","explain":"{a}x − {a}x = 0.","params":{"a":[1,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"(x + {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is k?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two middle boxes.","explain":"{a}x + {b}x = {=a+b}x.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"(x + {a})(x + {b}) = x² + kx + c. What is c?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply the numbers.","explain":"{a} × {b} = {=a*b}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many products before simplifying (x + 1)(x² + 2x + 3)?","choices":["6","5","3"],"answer":0,"hint":"2 terms × 3 terms.","explain":"2 × 3 = 6."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"(x − 1)(x² + x + 1) = ?","choices":["x³ − 1","x³ + 1","x³ − x − 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most terms cancel.","explain":"x³ + x² + x − x² − x − 1 = x³ − 1."}],"explain":{"kid":["(x + 2)(x + 3) means x + 2 lots of x + 3. Draw a grid: put x and 2 down the side, x and 3 along the top.","Fill each box by multiplying: x², 3x, 2x, 6. Add the like terms: x² + 5x + 6."],"adult":["Every term of the first bracket multiplies every term of the second. A binomial times a trinomial makes 2 × 3 = 6 products before you simplify.","(x − 1)(x² + x + 1) = x³ + x² + x − x² − x − 1 = x³ − 1. A grid keeps track of each product and its sign. For three brackets, multiply two first, then the third."]},"familyTip":"Multiply 12 × 13 with a grid: (10 + 2)(10 + 3). It's the same method as (x + 2)(x + 3)."},"mt_zt4q9lu5va":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"(5x² + x − 4) − (2x² − 3x + 1) = ?","choices":["3x² + 4x − 5","3x² − 2x − 3","3x² + 4x − 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flip every sign in the second.","explain":"5x² − 2x² = 3x², x + 3x = 4x, −4 − 1 = −5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"({a}x − {b}) − ({c}x − {d}). What is the constant term?","answer":"d-b","hint":"−{b} − (−{d}).","explain":"−{b} + {d} = {=d-b}.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,3],"d":[1,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"({a}x² + {b}x) + ({c}x² − {b}x). What is the x coefficient?","answer":"0","hint":"They cancel.","explain":"{b} − {b} = 0.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9],"c":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which pair are like terms?","choices":["4xy and −xy","x²y and xy²","3x and 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same letters, same powers.","explain":"4xy and −xy match exactly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"(x³ + 2) + (x² − 2) = ?","choices":["x³ + x²","2x⁵","x³ + x² + 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Nothing to combine except numbers.","explain":"2 − 2 = 0, so x³ + x²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"({a}x² + 1) − ({b}x² + 1). What is the x² coefficient?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[6,12],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A common mistake in (6x + 2) − (x − 4) is getting…","choices":["5x − 2","5x + 6","7x − 2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Forgetting to flip −4.","explain":"The right answer is 5x + 6; forgetting the sign gives 5x − 2."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"({a}x² + 2x) + ({b}x² + x). What is the x² coefficient?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add like terms.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"({a}x + 5) − ({b}x + 1). What is the x coefficient?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Subtract like terms.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}.","params":{"a":[5,12],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Are 3x² and 3x like terms?","choices":["No, the powers differ","Yes, both have 3","Yes, both have x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same letter AND same power.","explain":"x² and x have different powers."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"−(2x − 3) = ?","choices":["−2x + 3","−2x − 3","2x + 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change every sign.","explain":"−2x and +3."}],"explain":{"kid":["Like terms have the same letter and the same power, like 3x² and 5x². You can add them: 8x².","x² and x are not like terms. Keep them apart, like apples and oranges."],"adult":["To add, group like terms: (3x² + 2x − 1) + (x² − 5x + 4) = 4x² − 3x + 3.","To subtract, the minus sign hits every term in the second bracket. So (5x² + x − 4) − (2x² − 3x + 1) = 5x² + x − 4 − 2x² + 3x − 1 = 3x² + 4x − 5."]},"familyTip":"Write terms on sticky notes. Group the ones that are like terms, then add them up."}});
